# <<scenario_name>>

A glossogen scenario in its own project. Generated by `glossogen new-scenario`,
and runnable as generated: two agents relay a code word over a metered link, and
a round succeeds when the word gets across.

```text
pyproject.toml          # [tool.glossogen] scenarios = ["scenarios"]
scenarios/
├── <<scenario_name>>.py    # @scenario(name="<<scenario_name>>"), its knobs, event, world
├── knobs_default.json
└── prompts/
tests/
```

## Setup

```bash
python3.14 -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate
pip install -e ".[testing]"
cp .env.example .env      # then put your ANTHROPIC_API_KEY in it
```

glossogen needs Python 3.14, so make the venv from a 3.14 interpreter. One made
from an older `python` stops the install at `requires a different Python`.

glossogen finds the scenario through `[tool.glossogen] scenarios` in
`pyproject.toml`, read from the nearest `pyproject.toml` at or above the
directory you run a command in. It imports the files in `scenarios/` that use
`@scenario`, so a script or helper module beside them is never run. Run
`glossogen` from anywhere inside this project, or pass `--scenarios <path>`
before the subcommand from elsewhere.

`pyproject.toml` pins glossogen to `<<glossogen_ref>>`, the ref `new-scenario`
was given. That defaults to the release matching the version of the glossogen it
ran from, so if you generated this from a branch or a commit install, it names
the release that install is ahead of rather than what you are running. Change it
to your own ref if so.

## Check it

```bash
glossogen validate scenarios/<<scenario_name>>.py   # the contract, no API key
pytest                                          # the round loop, no API key needed
```

`validate` also checks that `package-data` covers the prompts and presets, which
only matters if you build a wheel of this project. Once the scenario loads,
`glossogen validate <<scenario_name>>` runs the same contract checks by name.

## Run it

```bash
glossogen run <<scenario_name>> \
  --config knobs_default \
  --model claude-haiku-4-5-20251001 --provider anthropic \
  --runs-dir ./runs
```

`--config` takes the name of a preset beside the scenario module, or a path to a
JSON file of your own. Add `round_count=5` or any other knob as trailing
`key=value` arguments to override the preset for one run.

## Look at it

```bash
glossogen serve --runs-dir ./runs --port 8000 --ui-port 3000
```

The web UI at <http://localhost:3000> lists the run, with the message timeline,
each agent's reasoning, and the round verdicts. `--ui-port` needs Docker; drop it
to serve the API alone.

## Making it yours

The generated scenario works so that you can change one thing at a time and see
what breaks. In rough order, all in `scenarios/<<scenario_name>>.py` unless named:

1. `prompts/*.jinja` and `scenario_description` — what the agents are told.
2. The ids and `<<scenario_name>>_teams` — who exists, what channels they have,
   and which tools each may call.
3. `<<class_prefix>>World` — what changes during a round, and what the agents are
   notified of.
4. `judge_round_result` — what counts as success. This is what the
   `round_success` metric reads.
5. `<<class_prefix>>Knobs` and `knobs_default.json` — what a run can vary.

Every event class the scenario logs is listed in `event_types()`.

Full guide: <https://github.com/agencyenterprise/GlossoGen/blob/main/docs/creating-a-scenario.md>
